---
/**
 * How it works — the pipeline in depth.
 *
 * Copy derives from docs/marketing/product-narrative.md and Tense discipline is
 * load-bearing here: the autonomous lane is LIVE and written in the present tense; removing the
 * approval step for bank-matched payments is NEXT and is labelled as such, never implied as done.
 */
import CTASection from '@/components/CTASection.astro'
import PageHero from '@/components/PageHero.astro'
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import ResolveText from '@/components/ResolveText.astro'

const steps = [
  {
    n: '01',
    name: 'Ingest',
    line: 'Read once, never twice',
    body: 'Credere reads your bank feed every few minutes and resumes from a saved cursor instead of re-sweeping history. A retried poll, a network blip, or a restart mid-batch produces no duplicate rows.',
    detail:
      'This is the least glamorous step and the one most in-house builds get wrong. Reading each transaction exactly once is what makes every decision after it safe to repeat.',
  },
  {
    n: '02',
    name: 'Match',
    line: 'Matched only on proof',
    body: 'Every transaction is matched to the invoice it belongs to, on confirmed evidence or not at all. Clearing the floor takes a reference the payment itself carries, checked against that invoice in your billing system. Nothing else counts.',
    detail:
      'Zero candidates is an exception. More than one is an exception. So is an unresolved sibling payment against the same invoice in the same batch. Credere does not break ties.',
  },
  {
    n: '03',
    name: 'Classify',
    line: 'Duplicate, overpayment, or clean',
    body: 'Matched transactions resolve to one of four types. Duplicate detection reads what Credere has confirmed itself. It never reads your billing system’s “paid” flag, which can be set for a dozen reasons Credere cannot see.',
    detail:
      'Reasoning about money from a flag you did not write and cannot explain is how wrong credits get issued. We read what we can prove.',
  },
  {
    n: '04',
    name: 'Act',
    line: 'A credit where one is owed',
    body: 'Duplicates and overpayments produce a credit note for the right amount. Normal payments are reconciled with no credit at all. The decision goes to its own record, labelled as automation.',
    detail:
      'It never touches the human decision record. No report can later present it as something a person signed off.',
  },
  {
    n: '05',
    name: 'Post',
    line: 'Written back, idempotently',
    body: 'The correction is queued and drained to your billing system by a separate worker. A write that fails is retried without risking a second posting.',
    detail:
      'The dangerous case is not a failed write. It is a write that succeeded while the acknowledgement went missing. That gets its own state instead of a blind retry.',
  },
  {
    n: '06',
    name: 'Report',
    line: 'Posted back to your books',
    body: 'At the end of the day you get two lists. Everything Credere settled, each with the basis for its decision. And the short list whose evidence did not settle.',
    detail:
      'The two cannot overlap. An item is either resolved or waiting for you. It is never presented as both.',
  },
]

const types = [
  { type: 'normal_payment', means: 'The payment settles the invoice it names', does: 'Reconciled. No credit.', tone: 'confirmed' },
  { type: 'duplicate', means: 'An earlier confirmed payment already settled this invoice', does: 'Credit note for the duplicated amount', tone: 'confirmed' },
  { type: 'overpayment', means: 'The payment exceeds what the invoice was for', does: 'Credit note for the excess', tone: 'confirmed' },
  { type: 'unmatchable', means: 'The evidence does not settle it', does: 'Routed to a person, with the reasoning attached', tone: 'exception' },
]
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How it works"
  description="Credere polls your bank feed, matches each payment to an invoice on confirmed evidence, classifies it, credits what is owed, and posts it back to your billing system. Anything ambiguous becomes an exception with the reasoning attached."
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="How it works"
    title="What happens between a payment landing and your books being right."
    lede="Credere sits between two systems you already have: your bank, and your billing system. Between them it runs the loop a person runs today. Six steps, and five of them need nobody."
  />

  {/* --------------------------------------------------------------- the two doors */}
  <section id="two-doors" class="section-y">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="grid gap-10 md:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="border-hairline bg-surface rounded-lg lift border p-8">
          <span class="chip chip-confirmed">primary</span>
          <h2 class="mt-5 text-2xl font-medium">The bank feed finds the work</h2>
          <p class="text-ink-muted mt-4 leading-relaxed">
            Nobody types anything. Payments arrive, Credere works out what each one was, and the ones
            it can prove are handled end to end, including the write-back. This is the engine. It
            runs whether or not anyone signs in.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="border-hairline bg-surface rounded-lg lift border p-8">
          <span class="chip chip-neutral">secondary</span>
          <h2 class="mt-5 text-2xl font-medium">A person asks, in plain language</h2>
          <p class="text-ink-muted mt-4 leading-relaxed">
            “Credit $2,000 on INV-1042, duplicate charge.” The agent pulls out the invoice, amount
            and reason, fetches the invoice, and checks it against policy before any draft exists.
            It is a real feature and it demos well. It is also the fallback, not the engine.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ----------------------------------------------------------------- the six steps */}
  <section id="pipeline" class="rule-top section-y field">
    <div class="shell">
      <span class="accent-bar mb-4" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <p class="label">The pipeline</p>
      <h2 class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-[clamp(1.8rem,3.2vw,2.6rem)] leading-[1.1] font-medium">
        Six steps, in order, every few minutes.
      </h2>

      <ol class="mt-16 space-y-px">
        {
          steps.map((step, i) => (
            <li
              class="border-hairline reveal-i grid gap-6 border-t py-10 md:grid-cols-[7rem_1fr_1fr] md:gap-10"
              style={`--i: ${i % 2}`}
            >
              <div>
                <p class="mono accent-resolve text-xs">{step.n}</p>
                <h3 class="mt-2 text-xl font-medium">{step.name}</h3>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed">{step.body}</p>
              </div>
              <div class="md:pt-1">
                <p class="text-ink-subtle text-xs font-semibold tracking-[0.08em] uppercase">{step.line}</p>
                <p class="text-ink-muted mt-3 leading-relaxed">{step.detail}</p>
              </div>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ------------------------------------------------------------ the matching floor */}
  <section id="matching-floor" class="rule-top section-y">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="grid gap-14 lg:grid-cols-[1fr_1fr]">
        <div>
          <span class="accent-bar mb-4" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <p class="label">The matching floor</p>
          <h2 class="mt-4 text-[clamp(1.8rem,3.2vw,2.6rem)] leading-[1.1] font-medium">
            The model advises. It never authorizes.
          </h2>
          <div class="text-ink-muted mt-7 space-y-5 text-lg leading-relaxed">
            <p>
              Bank narration is hostile to machines. <span class="mono text-ink text-base">WIRE
              IN NORTHWIND TRADING INV-1042</span> is a good day. A reference field holding nothing
              but a trace number is a normal one.
            </p>
            <p>
              A language model reads that narration and proposes a candidate. That proposal is
              <span class="text-ink font-medium"> advisory</span>. It can enrich an exception so the
              person reviewing it starts with context. It can never be the thing that moves money.
            </p>
            <ResolveText text="What can: a reference the payment itself carries, confirmed against the invoice it names. Everything above that floor is handled automatically. Everything below it becomes an exception." />
            <p class="text-ink">
              The whole product rests on one distinction: <span class="font-medium"
                >existence is not attribution</span
              >. Finding a number that looks like an invoice reference is not the same as knowing
              the payment belongs to that invoice.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div>
          <div class="border-hairline bg-surface rounded-lg lift border p-6 sm:p-8">
            <span class="accent-bar mb-4" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <p class="label">Classification</p>
            <ul class="mt-6 space-y-6">
              {
                types.map((c) => (
                  <li class="border-hairline grid gap-2 border-b pb-6 last:border-0 last:pb-0 sm:grid-cols-[auto_1fr] sm:gap-5">
                    <span class:list={['chip', c.tone === 'confirmed' ? 'chip-confirmed' : 'chip-exception']}>
                      {c.type}
                    </span>
                    <div>
                      <p class="text-sm">{c.means}</p>
                      <p class="text-ink-muted mt-1 text-sm">{c.does}</p>
                    </div>
                  </li>
                ))
              }
            </ul>
          </div>
          <p class="text-ink-subtle mt-5 text-sm leading-relaxed">
            Ambiguity resolves to <span class="mono">unmatchable</span>, never to a guess. A tie is
            not broken. It is escalated.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- the day, and next */}
  <section id="oversight" class="rule-top section-y field-alt">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="grid gap-14 lg:grid-cols-[1.1fr_0.9fr]">
        <div>
          <span class="accent-bar mb-4" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <p class="label">Oversight</p>
          <h2 class="mt-4 text-[clamp(1.8rem,3.2vw,2.6rem)] leading-[1.1] font-medium">
            Your 5:30pm, not your 9am.
          </h2>
          <div class="text-ink-muted mt-7 space-y-5 text-lg leading-relaxed">
            <p>
              Because every action is stamped at write time with what kind of actor took it, Credere
              can answer a question most finance tools cannot: what fraction of your money decisions
              had no human in the loop.
            </p>
            <p>
              Most systems cannot tell their own automation apart from their users. That turns “the
              system did this automatically” into an awkward discovery during an audit. Here it is a
              recorded fact you can hand over.
            </p>
            <p>
              Credere also watches for the failure nobody notices: a feed that is configured but not
              being processed. An empty queue looks like a quiet week right up until close.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="border-hairline bg-surface rounded-lg lift border p-8">
          <span class="chip chip-neutral">shipping next</span>
          <h3 class="mt-5 text-xl font-medium">
            Removing the approval step for bank-matched payments
          </h3>
          <p class="text-ink-muted mt-4 leading-relaxed">
            Where attribution is confirmed, Credere will classify, credit and post with no human gate
            before the write. The finance head keeps the end-of-day report and the exception queue.
          </p>
          <p class="text-ink-muted mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
            That would be reckless if approval were the only control. It never was the strongest
            one. A person rubber-stamping a queue of confident matches is approving the same
            evidence the system already confirmed. The matching floor, idempotent writes, the
            recoverable posting queue, honest audit and the exception route all stay. Discretionary
            credits keep a human decision, because there the evidence is a commercial judgement
            rather than a fact about a payment.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTASection />
</BaseLayout>
